<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Definitions xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema">
	<CubeBlocks>
		
		<Definition>
			<Id>
				<TypeId>EnvironmentDefinition</TypeId>
				<SubtypeId>DefaultThermodynamicsLoop</SubtypeId>
			</Id>
			<ModExtensions>
				<Group Name="ThermalLoopProperties">
					<!--
						kg of coolant per cubic metre of the cell a pipe occupies, so a ring's charge
						scales with its length AND with the grid it is built on. Per pipe rather than
						per loop: a fixed charge per loop divided the same fluid into smaller parcels
						as a ring grew, which made every parcel stiffer to integrate and made a longer
						ring couple harder to the grid while holding no more coolant.

						A density rather than a flat mass, because a flat mass has no grid size in it.
						This was 50 kg a pipe until C43: 3.2 kg/m3 in a 2.5 m cube, which is a gas,
						against 400 kg/m3 in a 0.5 m one, which is a liquid and outweighs the 32 kg
						pipe block carrying it. 33 kg/m3 is a bore a fifth of the cell across filled
						with water-glycol - 515.6 kg a pipe on a large grid, 4.1 kg on a small one.

						CoolantMassPerPipe is still read and still means kilograms in a pipe. State it
						instead of this to pin a flat charge; state neither and this default applies.
					-->
					<Decimal Name="CoolantKilogramsPerCubicMetre" Value ="33" />
					<!--
						W/(m2 K): how well heat crosses between the fluid and the wall it touches,
						WHILE THE RING IS CIRCULATING. Convective, so there is no thickness in it -
						the resistance is the boundary layer against the wall. It was a 0..1 quality
						against a reference conductivity divided by half a cell, which gave the game
						a second conduction pace and made the coefficient it implied five times
						larger on a small grid than on a large one, for the same fluid against the
						same wall. See backlog.md, C20.

						1000 because a pumped ring is forced convection. 160 was what the loop
						already ran at before it was restated as a coefficient, and it is the
						stagnant end of the range - a water-glycol mix moved by a pump is a few
						hundred to a few thousand in any handbook. It is now the value a STOPPED
						ring runs at, through StagnantTransferFraction below, so nothing behaves
						worse than it did and what changed is that running the pump buys something.

						This is the only dial on the pickup, because the sink face count is fixed by
						design (blocks.md), and the pickup decides whether a block has an answer at
						all: watts over it is the gradient a buried source is forced to sit at
						however much radiator is hung off the far end. At 160 a 6.4 MW jump drive
						forced 6,400 K against a 689 K rating on one sink face and 1,600 K on four,
						so no realistic plumbing could cool it and the mod's own design thesis could
						not be built. See balance.md, What a jump drive costs in radiator, and
						backlog.md C42.
					-->
					<Decimal Name="HeatTransferCoefficient" Value="1000" />
					<!--J/(kg K), real units: water-glycol coolant-->
					<Decimal Name="SpecificHeat" Value="3400" />
					<Decimal Name="PipeContactMultiplier" Value ="1" />
					<Decimal Name="SinkContactMultiplier" Value ="1" />
					<!--
						How fast the coolant moves with one pump at full power, metres per second —
						the same unit the terminal reports. This is how fast heat travels round the
						ring, and with it how long a radiator takes to hear about a reactor.

						Flow rises with the square root of combined pumping, so four pumps carry
						twice this, not four times.

						Split by grid size because it is a balance dial, not a physical constant: a
						small-grid pump is a much smaller machine driving a much shorter ring, and
						whether it should move its coolant as fast as a capital ship's is a
						judgement. Shipped equal, so the two grids behave alike until someone
						decides they should not.

						A small-grid pipe is a fifth as long, so the same speed is five times the
						parcel rate: the ring laps far more often and levels out sooner.
					-->
					<Decimal Name="LargeGridFlowRate" Value ="10" />
					<Decimal Name="SmallGridFlowRate" Value ="10" />
					<!--
						What survives with nothing circulating, as a share of the coefficient above.
						Fluid-to-wall transfer is convective, so it depends on the flow: a pumped
						ring is forced convection and a stopped one is natural convection against
						the same wall, the best part of an order of magnitude apart.

						0.16 because 1000 x 0.16 is 160, which is exactly what every ring in this mod
						ran at before C42 - so a stopped ring is unchanged and no situation anywhere
						is worse than it was. What the pair buys is that running the pump now makes
						the ring CONDUCT rather than only mixing it, which is a reason to switch a
						pump on and to pay for the power it draws.

						Until C37 this field multiplied nothing at all: it described the transport
						between parcels, which Advect already stops dead on zero flow.
					-->
					<Decimal Name="StagnantTransferFraction" Value ="0.16" />
				</Group>
			</ModExtensions>
		</Definition>

	</CubeBlocks>
</Definitions>
